        <div id="allergy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Allergen-Friendly, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            OWYN's clearest advantage is allergy positioning. The brand says its products are free from the top nine allergens, which in the US are milk, eggs, fish, shellfish, tree nuts, peanuts, wheat, soybeans and sesame, and its pages carry FARE certification. FARE, Food Allergy Research and Education, is the largest US food allergy advocacy organization.
          </p>

          <p>
            For someone managing a milk or soy allergy, that is a meaningful shortcut. Many plant powders avoid dairy but use soy, and many use shared facilities with peanuts or tree nuts. OWYN's pea, pumpkin, chia and flax base avoids the main problem ingredients, and its allergen claims are central to the brand rather than a footnote.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Allergy Note</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read the current label every time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Formulas and facilities change. If you or a child has a severe allergy, check the allergen statement on the exact product you are buying, and talk to your allergist or doctor before relying on any brand's allergy claims. This page is not medical advice.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The pea and pumpkin combination is also nutritionally reasonable. Pea protein is relatively low in methionine, and pumpkin seed protein brings a different amino acid profile that helps round it out, in the same way that rice complements pea in many other blends. At 30 g per serving, Pro Elite supplies plenty of total essential amino acids for most adults.
          </p>

          <p>
            What OWYN does not offer is sport-specific certification. Its pages show Non-GMO, FARE and, for some shakes, kosher badges, but not NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport at the time of writing. Plant proteins also tend to carry more trace heavy metals than dairy proteins because the crops absorb them from soil, which is why certifications and published test results matter more in this category. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains how to weigh that risk.
          </p>
        </div>

