            10. Who Should Skip OWYN
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Drug-tested athletes.</strong> OWYN does not advertise NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification at the time of writing. Choose a product listed in those databases, such as Garden of Life Sport or Vega Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>People who want organic.</strong> OWYN is Non-GMO but not USDA Organic as a range, although some ingredients in the shakes are organic. If organic certification matters to you, Orgain, Garden of Life and others fit better. Our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">best organic protein powder</a> guide lists the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Anyone who wants a single-ingredient powder.</strong> Both OWYN powders include gums, flavors, a probiotic and a greens blend. That is fine in a shake but less useful if you want to bake with protein or keep your ingredient list short. A plain pea isolate is the better tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buyers who only drink bottles.</strong> If you never mix powder, OWYN shakes are still a good allergy-friendly choice, but expect to pay about $3.25 to $4 per 25 g of protein. Buy multi-packs when you find them on sale.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>People with medical conditions.</strong> If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, have food allergies or take medication, speak to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. Nothing on this page is medical advice.
