                OWYN Pro Elite Protein Powder lists 30 g of protein per two-scoop serving, and the original OWYN Protein Powder lists 20 g per two-scoop serving, per the chocolate nutrition panels at the time of writing. Check the panel on your tub, since figures vary slightly by flavor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is OWYN protein dairy-free and vegan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. OWYN products are plant-based and dairy-free, built on pea protein with pumpkin, chia or flax depending on the product. OWYN says its products are free from the top nine allergens and carries FARE certification. People with severe allergies should still read each label and consult their allergist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What sweetener does OWYN use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pro Elite powder uses monk fruit extract and has no sugar. The original powder and original shakes use organic cane sugar plus monk fruit, at about 4 g of added sugar per serving. The Pro Elite shake uses monk fruit and stevia. None of the products reviewed here use sucralose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns OWYN?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                OWYN is owned by The Simply Good Foods Company, which also owns the Quest and Atkins brands, per its corporate website. OWYN stands for Only What You Need. Its products are sold through large grocery chains, Amazon and OWYN's own website, so prices vary by channel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the OWYN shake or powder better value?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The powder, by a wide margin. Pro Elite powder costs about $1.55 per 25 g of protein at list price, while OWYN shakes cost roughly $3.25 to $4 per 25 g. Shakes are worth it for convenience, but if you drink one daily at home, the tub saves a lot of money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is OWYN third-party tested for sport?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                OWYN's product pages show FARE, Non-GMO and, for some shakes, kosher badges, but not NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification at the time of writing. Drug-tested athletes should choose a plant protein listed in those databases, such as Garden of Life Sport Organic Plant-Based Protein.
              </p>
            </div>

